    "title": "Texas A&M baseball vs Texas State game score: Aggies fall to Bobcats",
    "text": "For the first time this season, Texas A&M baseball lost a midweek game, falling Tuesday to Texas State 9-8.The Aggies (25-7, 7-5 SEC) took an early lead, scoring three runs in the first two innings. The Bobcats (22-10, 8-4 Sun Belt) fought back with three runs in the bottom of the second and added three more in the third, taking a 6-3 lead.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢Scoring two runs in the fourth and another two in the sixth, A&M regained the advantage, 7-6. However, the Bobcats mounted a late-inning rally, scoring three runs in the seventh to take a lead they would never relinquish.Texas A&M infielder Nico Partida during a game in the 2026 season.Texas A&M AthleticsThe Aggies' 2-3-4 hitters stepped to the plate in the eighth inning down two. Texas State right-hander Cade Smith struck out center fielder Caden Sorrell and second baseman Chris Hacopian. Left fielder Jake Duer slapped a single, which chased Smith. Right-hander Wade Cooper recorded the final four outs for his fifth save.Article continues below this adA&M struck out two more times against Cooper in the ninth inning, ending the game with a total of 10 strikeouts. Texas State outhit the Aggies 13-10; A&M stranded 11 compared to TSU’s six.Aggies’ reliever Cole Hubert was used as a starter before they turned the game into a bullpen outing. The left-hander pitched two innings and gave up three runs and four hits. He struck out two and did not give up a walk on 39 pitches.Five Aggies pitchers were deployed in the outing, with the four relievers allowing six runs, nine hits and issuing three walks.Article continues below this adBobcats’ starting pitcher, Alizaeh Gutierrez, had similar struggles. The lefty pitched two innings, giving up three runs and three hits. He struck out one and allowed one walk on 41 pitches.A&M came into the game ranked 18th in the nation by D1baseball.com. Next up for the Aggies is a 7 p.m. Friday game against No. 2 Texas to Blue Bell Park.Final updates from the action: Texas A&M baseball vs Texas State: Scoring updates from Tuesday Final: Texas State 9, Texas A&M 8 End 8: Texas State 9, Texas A&M 7 Back-to-back singles put two on for the Bobcats. From there, Darden settled down to strike out three consecutive hitters to get out of the jam.Article continues below this ad Mid 8: Texas State 9, Texas A&M 7 With their 2-3-4 hitters due up, Sorrell and Hacopian struck out before Duer ripped a single up the middle. But that was all the offense the Aggies had in the inning. End 7: Texas State 9, Texas A&M 7 A pair of walks put two on with one out before the Aggies turned to relievers Juan Vargas and Ethan Darden. A two-out double by Farris tied the game before a single by Stewart in the next at-bat pushed across two more to give the Bobcats the lead 9-7.Article continues below this ad Mid 7: Texas A&M 7, Texas State 6 Harrison ripped a one-out double to left field, but the Aggies left him stranded. End 6: Texas A&M 7, Texas State 6 A two-out single was the only offense Texas State had in the inning as A&M reliever Grant Cunningham struck out two batters in the frame.Article continues below this ad Mid 6: Texas A&M 7, Texas State 6 Grahovac reached with an E5, followed by a Sorrell walk put two on with no outs. From there, Hacopian slapped a single through the left side to tie the game. Partida then doubled to right-center field to give the Aggies the lead back, 7-6. End 5: Texas State 6, Texas A&M 5 Galloway opened the inning with a double, but that was all the offense the Bobcats mustered in the inning.Article continues below this ad Mid 5: Texas State 6, Texas A&M 5 Wesley Jordan pinch hit for Binderup and struck out swinging on three pitches. From there, Harrison scraped to a two-out single, but that was all for the offense. End 4: Texas State 6, Texas A&M 5 A leadoff walk was erased as the Aggies got out of the inning unscathed thanks to a 3-6 double play.Article continues below this ad Mid 4: Texas State 6, Texas A&M 5 Harrison drew a leadoff walk, which led Kellner to crush a no-doubt home run to left field, bringing the Aggies' deficit down to one. End 3: Texas State 6, Texas A&M 3 An error by Hacopian allowed Galloway to reach second before Park singled to right field, putting runners on the corners with no outs. From there, Farris hit a sacrifice fly to center field, driving in a run to give Texas State the lead. Stewart then blasted a two-run home run to center field to push the lead to 6-3Article continues below this ad Mid 3: Texas A&M 3, Texas State 3 Duer and Partida began the inning with a pair of walks, but a Binderup strikeout was followed by a Wilson double play that ended it. End 2: Texas A&M 3, Texas State 3 Salas responded with a leadoff solo home run to left field. From there, the Bobcats loaded the bases, which allowed Vossos to hit a two-out double that tied the game at 3-3.Article continues below this ad Mid 2: Texas A&M 3, Texas State 0 Grahovac added another run with a one-out solo blast to left-center field. End 1: Texas A&M 2, Texas State 0 An infield single was the only offense the Bobcats mustered in the inning.Article continues below this ad Mid 1: Texas A&M 2, Texas State 0 A fielding error put Grahovac on before Sorrell's double down the left field line drove in the game's first run. A Duer single drove in Sorrell to give A&M the 2-0 lead. Texas A&M 1B Gavin Grahovac CF Caden Sorrell 2B Chris Hacopian LF Jake Duer 3B Nico Partida DH Blake Binderup RF Jorian Wilson C Bear Harrison SS Boston Kellner Article continues below this ad Texas State CF Jackson Cotton RF Rashawn Galloway SS Dawson Park DH Manny Salas 1B Ethan Farris LF Jaquae Stewart 3B Chase Mora C Clayton Namken 2B Justin Vossos Texas A&M starting pitcher LHP Cole Hubert Texas State starting pitcher Alizaeg Gutierrez Texas A&M baseball vs. Texas State: How to watch Tuesday's game When: 6 p.m. TuesdayArticle continues below this adWhere: Bobcat Ballpark, San MarcosTV/radio: ESPN+Reach Texas A&M beat reporter Tony Catalina via email at Anthony.Catalina@statesman.com.Article continues below this adGet the hookup and your Horns Up with Longhorns coverage.April 7, 2026Tony Catalina is the Texas A&M beat reporter for the Austin American-Statesman. He is a Heisman Trophy voter from Worcester, Mass., with more than five years of sports reporting experience. He previously worked at SB Nation, Pro Football Network and New England Football Journal, where he covered NFL, college and high school football before joining the Statesman. Before pursuing a career in journalism, Catalina served as a high school football coach in Massachusetts and, in 2016, was a recruiting analyst for the University of Georgia football program under head coach Kirby Smart.",
